{"id":"~Zhuoyang_Liu1","name":"Zhuoyang Liu","counts":{"accepted_papers":2,"first_position_papers":0,"co_first_author_papers":null,"corresponding_author_papers":null,"Poster only":2},"papers":[{"id":"xc8asDq7QI","title":"FTP-1: A Generalist Foundation Tactile Policy Across Tactile Sensors for Contact-Rich Manipulation","abstract":"Despite the success of vision-based generalist robotic policies, existing tactile-based policies remain tied to fixed embodiments and sensor setups. This is because tactile signals are highly heterogeneous across hardware, making cross-sensor generalization difficult. We present FTP-1, the first generalist foundation tactile policy pretrained to acquire transferable tactile manipulation abilities across diverse sensors and embodiments. FTP-1 supports varied tactile inputs, including image-, array-, and state-based signals, by using heterogeneous encoders to project them into unified morphology-aware latent tokens that are jointly modeled by a shared tactile Transformer expert. Pretrained on ~ 3,000 hours of tactile manipulation data aggregated from 26 data sources, spanning human and robot demonstrations across 21 sensors, FTP-1 learns tactile skills that transfer beyond the sensors seen during pretraining. Across downstream finetuning experiments spanning 5 hardware configurations, FTP-1 improves contact-rich manipulation on seen sensor setups by +17.2% and, surprisingly, transfers to two previously unseen tactile-sensor setups, achieving a +31% gain in success rate. FTP-1 establishes the first unified foundation baseline for tactile manipulation, providing future tactile policies with a shared model-level starting point. All model checkpoints, datasets, and training code will be released in the future.","summary":null,"type":"Poster only","topics":["模仿与示范学习","数据与评测","触觉与接触","安全、泛化与鲁棒性"],"title_topics":["触觉与接触"],"authors":["~Chengbo_Yuan2","~Zicheng_Zhang8","~Mingjie_Zhou2","~Wendi_Chen1","~Yi_Wang115","~Zhuoyang_Liu1","~Dantong_Niu1","~Shuo_Wang39","~Hui_Zhang31","~Wenkang_Zhang3","~Yingdong_Hu1","~Yuanqing_Gong1","~Wanli_Xing2","~Chuan_Wen1","~Cewu_Lu3","~Kaifeng_Zhang1","~Yang_Gao1"],"institutions":["ETH Zurich","Fudan University","Shanghai Innovation Institute","Shanghai Jiao Tong University","Shanghai Qi Zhi Institute","Sharpa","Tsinghua University","University of California, Berkeley"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.13102","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=xc8asDq7QI","project":[],"code":[],"poster_date":"2026-11-10","poster_time":"5:00–6:30 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":142,"hardware":[{"model":"Dexmate / Vega-1","env":["real"],"reviewed":true},{"model":"Sharpa 灵巧手 / Wave","env":["real"],"reviewed":true}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"xc8asDq7QI","position":6,"first":false,"roles":[]}},{"id":"KdaClHHEUt","title":"T-Rex: Tactile-Reactive Dexterous Manipulation","abstract":"The ability to react dynamically to tactile signals has long been considered crucial to agile human-level dexterity. Yet contemporary learning-based VLAs for robotic manipulation generally either overlook the tactile modality or are limited to encoders with static cues, in part due to  the scarcity of diverse training data and standardized evaluation, architectural constraints in current Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models, and limitations of static tactile encoders. In this paper, we push the frontier of tactile-reactive manipulation addressing all of these limitations. We open-source a large-scale, 100-hour tactile-rich dataset collected via a novel, data-efficient recipe that prioritizes elementary motor primitives. To effectively exploit naturally high-frequency touch signals without sacrificing the existing capabilities of existing VLAs, we introduce a variable-rate Mix-of-Transformer (MoT) architecture equipped with a novel temporal tactile VQ-VAE encoder. We demonstrate the effectiveness of tactile-reactive policies on 12 manipulation tasks requiring delicate force control, deformable object manipulation, achieving over 30% higher average success rate than the strongest baseline.","summary":"围绕接触状态快速变化的灵巧操作，将触觉反馈用于反应式动作修正。研究多阶段训练如何帮助策略在手物接触中及时调整，而不只依赖视觉与预定动作。","type":"Poster only","topics":["视觉语言动作与基础模型","数据与评测","触觉与接触","灵巧操作与机器人手"],"title_topics":["触觉与接触","灵巧操作与机器人手"],"authors":["~Dantong_Niu1","~Zhuoyang_Liu1","~Zekai_Wang6","~Boning_Shao1","~Zhao-Heng_Yin1","~Anirudh_Pai1","~Yuvan_Sharma1","~Stefano_Saravalle1","~Ruijie_Zheng1","~Jing_Wang79","~Ryan_Punamiya1","~Mengda_Xu1","~Yuqi_Xie1","~Yunfan_Jiang1","~Letian_Fu1","~Konstantinos_Kallidromitis1","~Matteo_Gioia1","~Junyi_Zhang3","~Jiaxin_Ge1","~Haiwen_Feng1","~Fabio_Galasso1","~Wei_Zhan2","~David_M._Chan1","~Yutong_Bai1","~Roei_Herzig2","~Jiahui_Lei1","~Li_Fei-Fei1","~Ken_Goldberg1","~Jitendra_Malik2","~Pieter_Abbeel2","~Yuke_Zhu1","~Danfei_Xu1","~Linxi_Fan2","~Trevor_Darrell2"],"institutions":["ItalAI","NVIDIA","Panasonic","Sapienza University of Rome","Stanford University","University of California, Berkeley"],"pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.17055","openreview":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=KdaClHHEUt","project":["https://tactile-rex.github.io","https://tactile-reactive-dexterous.github.io"],"code":["https://github.com/ZhuoyangLiu2005/T-Rex"],"poster_date":"2026-11-11","poster_time":"10:30 AM–12:00 PM","poster_venue":"JW Marriott","poster_number":135,"hardware":[{"model":"Dexmate / Vega-1","env":["real"],"reviewed":true},{"model":"Sharpa 灵巧手 / Wave","env":["real"],"reviewed":true},{"model":"Manus 手套","env":["real"],"reviewed":false},{"model":"HTC VIVE Tracker","env":["real"],"reviewed":false},{"model":"ZED 系列（2 / X / Mini）","env":["real"],"reviewed":true}],"has_social_sample":false,"role_evidence":null,"author_role":{"id":"KdaClHHEUt","position":2,"first":false,"roles":[]}}],"topics":{"数据与评测":2,"触觉与接触":2,"模仿与示范学习":1,"安全、泛化与鲁棒性":1,"视觉语言动作与基础模型":1,"灵巧操作与机器人手":1},"role_counts":{},"role_papers_reviewed":0,"profile":null,"portfolio":null,"needs_identif